ue] [17592186368741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368748 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368754 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368760 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1089 "q-334709" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1100 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1113 #uuid "b4af4f8d-8886-57f9-92e8-20fda99f6128" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368741 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =4(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.027-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.108-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.099\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.1) & =4(0.1)^{3}-(0.1)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.001-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.004-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.003\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.099\\,9$ to $0.003\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.099\\,9}{0.003\\,9}=25.615\\,38…≈25.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈25.615\\,38… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(25.615\\,38…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈2.952… \\\\ p & ≈1.952….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368743 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368744 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368745 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368746 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368747 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1041 "field-1" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368743 13194139535829 true] [17592186368742 1110 #uuid "e87f74e6-d992-5bee-ba58-d63d1a5c43ef" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368743 1034 "0.100" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535829 true] [17592186368743 1111 #uuid "1ff11c9b-c433-5eac-9106-369e77a125f4" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368744 1034 "0.050" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535829 true] [17592186368744 1111 #uuid "a953d5aa-5044-5beb-8e46-34fb86d932e4" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368745 1034 "0.375" 13194139535829 true] [17592186368745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1319413